    {
      "exampleValues": [
        "Although Mayaguez [MMI VIII] was not affected by the brunt of the waves, Figure 12 shows the now familiar photo of a house uplifted by the tsunami that flooded ashore there. Figure 13 pictures a small house carried seaward by the powerful backwash and left stranded near the shore. The\r\nretreating torrent is often as formidable as the initial upsurge of the wave and filled with all manner of debris. About 23 minutes after the earthquake, seawater entered the lower floors of buildings near the waterfront and destroyed a few native huts along the beach (Reid and Taber, 1999a, ref #1078). Watermarks left on houses indicated runups to 1.5 meters above sea level. Debris made many streets impassable, impeding fire trucks and rescue efforts. More than 100 people perished. (reference #1492)",
        "Waves were observed at Hoh River, Washington when the water rose in mouth of river. Validity of this runup event is 0.   (Ref #237)\r\n<P>The water rose in Grays Bay, and in the mouths of the Queet Quinault, Wishkah and Hoh Rivers (Reference #415).",
        "<P><blockquote><i>Reference #414:</blockquote><i>\r\n<P>The pen stopped on the tide gauge at Ayukawa after the beginning of the earthquake, as a result of the strong oscillations. The record resumed 6 hours later. The record showed that the sea level was oscillating in a way typical of tsunamis. The mean period of oscillations was 20-25 minutes and the maximal amplitude was 5 cm. The assumed travel time of the tsunami to Ayukawa was 15 minutes.",
        "The maximal amplitude (range) of oscillations in level is 0.1 m and the travel time is 0:44 (Reference #414).",
        "A strong surge in the bay affected the port buildings and loading docks."
      ],
